  "english": "A BRIEF PREAMBLE\nCONCERNING THE SECOND VOLUME OF THIS TONATSUYTS [DIRECTORY]\n... [untranslatable string of index letters]\n\nFirst, let it be known that:\nThis Second Volume was created as a rule and guide for the First Volume.\nBecause those intervals of time which increase and decrease, and whose specific details the First Volume cannot display,\nas well as the feasts that fall within such intervals and, being movable, cannot be celebrated in the same place [every year]—\nthis Second Volume displays these, regulated in all details, as you can see.\nBut those intervals and feasts which are immovable, and which are set down in the First Volume, always remain unchanged in the same way;\nthis Second Volume does not mention or display them, but instructs you to look in the First Volume in each respective place, as you can see.\nYet, within these intervals, certain feasts that are sometimes relocated due to the requirement of the Calendar Letter, and do not remain the same,\nthis Second Volume lists by name and mentions.\nTherefore, do not be confused by such things; for whatever this Second Volume mentions,\nknow that these things are altered in that year.\nIt also mentions the [hymns of the] Fathers [Harts], the days of the month, and the daily tones, so that you may know them.\nAnd whatever it leaves out and does not mention, you must look up in the First Volume and proceed according to the order.\nFor this reason, it is written in many places in this Second Volume: \"See Volume 1,\" preserving the order of the place,\nso that you may know that you must always keep the table of the Calendar Letter in hand alongside this Second Volume,\nand proceed according to its indications with the First Volume.\n\nSecond: This Second Volume instructs you to look for certain intervals of some calendar letters under other letters,\nas you can see under B, C, E, and so on.\nThe reason for this is that if we had put everything under every letter, there would have been much repetition, and the Tonatsuyts book would have grown too large.\nTherefore, just as we directed you to look in the First Volume for the immovable feasts,\nlikewise, concerning certain movable intervals and feasts,\nif they were found to be identical in form under some letters,\nwe placed them in full under one calendar letter in this Second Volume,\nand under the other matching letters, we wrote to look under that letter, in which there is no difficulty.\n\nThird: In this Second Volume, under some letters, we have written brief instructions regarding leap years.\nYou must observe those instructions only in that leap year in which those same letters are the calendar letters, and not in other years.\nFor example, under the calendar letter T, it is written in small cursive script:\n\"When a leap year occurs, and the letter of the year is 19, on this Saturday do not celebrate [Saint] Theodosius,\" etc.\nYou must observe this instruction only in that year which is a leap year and when the letter of the year is 19.\nBut in other years, whether leap year or not, celebrate without hesitation just as is set down under each respective letter.\nKeep this same rule for all letters where such instructions are written concerning the leap letters.\n\nFourth: At the beginning of whichever table the daily tones are not placed,\nyou must maintain the order of the tones from the day of the Eve of Theophany [Birth],\nand the [hymns of the] Fathers [Harts] of the Martyrs and of the Fasts, and the Ascension [Hambartsi] hymns of the Resurrection,\nup to the Sunday of Real Good Friday [Great Lent Eve].\nFor it was not possible to place them at the beginning of every letter.",
